From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 12:00:00
Subject: [PATCH] guarded windows deleted while a screen goes away

QWindowPrivate::emitScreenChangedRecursion() emits screenChanged() and
then walks q->children() to recurse into the child windows. Both steps run
application code synchronously, and that code is free to delete windows: a
screenChanged() handler that closes a panel deletes the very window the
signal was emitted for, and a handler that unwinds an object lifetime can
delete a QObject child of the window being iterated. The loop binds a
reference to the live QObjectPrivate::children list, so a child removed
during the recursion invalidates the enclosing loop's iterators, and a
window removed during the emit leaves "q" itself dangling.

The same pattern sits on the screen removal path, where leftover windows
are moved to the new primary screen. QGuiApplication::allWindows() is
snapshotted as raw pointers, and setScreen()/setVisible() then run exactly
the handlers described above between iterations, so entries later in the
snapshot can already be freed by the time they are dereferenced.

This was seen live on Windows: replacing the Intel display driver under a
running process removes and re-adds every monitor, and the crash lands in
emitScreenChangedRecursion() reading a non-canonical pointer out of a
reused block right after the screenChanged() emission.

Hold the window under a QPointer across the emit, recurse over a snapshot
of guarded child pointers, and guard the leftover window snapshot the same
way. Nothing changes when no window is destroyed.

Here the leftover window loop lives in QScreen::~QScreen().

diff --git a/src/gui/kernel/qscreen.cpp b/src/gui/kernel/qscreen.cpp
index 990272b0c25..3f2c37be497 100644
--- a/src/gui/kernel/qscreen.cpp
+++ b/src/gui/kernel/qscreen.cpp
@@ -45,6 +45,7 @@
 #include <qpa/qplatformscreen_p.h>
 
 #include <QtCore/QDebug>
+#include <QtCore/QPointer>
 #include <QtCore/private/qobject_p.h>
 #include "qhighdpiscaling_p.h"
 
@@ -156,13 +157,27 @@ QScreen::~QScreen()
         && newPrimaryScreen->handle()->virtualSiblings().contains(handle());
 
     // Move any leftover windows to the primary screen
+    // Patched: setScreen() emits screenChanged() and setVisible() delivers
+    // show and geometry events, both of which run application code that can
+    // close and delete other windows still waiting in this snapshot.
+    // allWindows() hands out raw pointers, so hold them through QPointer and
+    // re-check every one before it is dereferenced.
     const auto allWindows = QGuiApplication::allWindows();
-    for (QWindow *window : allWindows) {
+    QList<QPointer<QWindow>> windows;
+    windows.reserve(allWindows.size());
+    for (QWindow *window : allWindows)
+        windows.append(window);
+    for (const QPointer<QWindow> &guard : windows) {
+        QWindow *window = guard.data();
+        if (!window)
+            continue;
         if (!window->isTopLevel() || window->screen() != this)
             continue;
 
         const bool wasVisible = window->isVisible();
         window->setScreen(newPrimaryScreen);
+        if (!guard)
+            continue;
 
         // Re-show window if moved from a virtual sibling screen. Otherwise
         // leave it up to the application developer to show the window.
diff --git a/src/gui/kernel/qwindow.cpp b/src/gui/kernel/qwindow.cpp
index a70555cae7c..e8328bd98f2 100644
--- a/src/gui/kernel/qwindow.cpp
+++ b/src/gui/kernel/qwindow.cpp
@@ -63,6 +63,7 @@
 
 #include <QtCore/QTimer>
 #include <QtCore/QDebug>
+#include <QtCore/QPointer>
 
 #include <QStyleHints>
 #include <qpa/qplatformcursor.h>
@@ -488,10 +489,28 @@ void QWindowPrivate::connectToScreen(QScreen *screen)
 void QWindowPrivate::emitScreenChangedRecursion(QScreen *newScreen)
 {
     Q_Q(QWindow);
+    // Patched: screenChanged() runs application code synchronously, and that
+    // code is free to delete windows - a handler that closes a panel deletes
+    // this very window, and an unwinding object lifetime can delete a QObject
+    // child of it. The loop below binds a reference to the live
+    // QObjectPrivate::children list and walks it across those calls, so both
+    // "q" and the list being iterated can die underneath it. Guard "q" with a
+    // QPointer and recurse over a snapshot of guarded child pointers.
+    QPointer<QWindow> guard(q);
     emit q->screenChanged(newScreen);
+    if (!guard)
+        return;
+    QList<QPointer<QWindow>> childWindows;
     for (QObject *child : q->children()) {
         if (child->isWindowType())
-            static_cast<QWindow *>(child)->d_func()->emitScreenChangedRecursion(newScreen);
+            childWindows.append(static_cast<QWindow *>(child));
+    }
+    for (const QPointer<QWindow> &child : childWindows) {
+        if (!child)
+            continue;
+        child->d_func()->emitScreenChangedRecursion(newScreen);
+        if (!guard)
+            return;
     }
 }
 
